E-APACHE】  | 【89†LICENSE-APACHE】  |    |\nL203: 【90†LICENSE-MIT】  | 【90†LICENSE-MIT】  |    |\nL204: 【91†README.md】  | 【91†README.md】  |    |\nL205: 【92†SECURITY.md】  | 【92†SECURITY.md】  |    |\nL206: [Button: View all files]\nL207: ## Repository files navigation\nL208: \nL209:   *   * 【93†README】\nL210:   * 【93†Apache-2.0 license】\nL211:   * 【93†MIT license】\nL212:   * 【93†Security】\nL213: \nL214: More items\nL215: # Efficient Sumcheck\nL216: \nL217: A high-performance sumcheck library with 【94†correctness-fuzzing】 against a verified oracle.\nL218: \nL219:   * Efficient — transparent SIMD acceleration (8-wide AVX-512, 2-wide NEON)\nL220:   * Streaming-capable — optional sublinear memory via sequential evaluation\nL221:   * Complete — built-in Fiat-Shamir, partial execution, per-round hooks\nL222: \nL223: Built using 【95†arkworks】. Compatible with any ecosystem — see 【96†`docs/compatibility.md`】.\nL224: \nL225: ## Quick Start\nL226: ### Multilinear Sumcheck\nL227: \nL228: Proves $H = \\sum_{x \\in \\lbrace 0,1 \\rbrace^v} p(x)$ where $p$ is a multilinear polynomial.\nL229: \nL230:     use effsc::{noop_hook, runner::sumcheck};\nL231:     use effsc::provers::multilinear::MultilinearProver;\nL232: \nL233:     let mut prover = MultilinearProver::new(evals);\nL234:     let proof = sumcheck(\nL235:         &mut prover,\nL236:         num_vars,\nL237:         &mut transcript,\nL238:         noop_hook,\nL239:     );\nL240: ### Inner Product Sumcheck\nL241: \nL242: Proves $H = \\sum_{x \\in \\lbrace 0,1 \\rbrace^v} f(x) \\cdot g(x)$ for two multilinear polynomials. Degree-2 round polynomials.\nL243: \nL244:     use effsc::{noop_hook, runner::sumcheck};\nL245:     use effsc::provers::inner_product::InnerProductProver;\nL246: \nL247:     let mut prover = InnerProductProver::new(a, b);\nL248:     let proof = sumcheck(\nL249:         &mut prover,\nL250:         num_vars,\nL251:         &mut transcript,\nL252:         noop_hook,\nL253:     );\nL254: ### Coefficient Sumcheck\nL255: Proves $H = \\sum_{x \\in \\lbrace 0,1 \\rbrace^v} p(x)$ where $\\deg_{x_i}(p) \\leq d$. The user implements `RoundPolyEvaluator` to define per-pair round polynomial contributions; the library handles iteration, parallelism, and reductions.\nL256: \nL257:     use effsc::{noop_hook, runner::sumcheck};\nL258:     use effsc::provers::coefficient::CoefficientProver;\nL259: \nL260:     let mut prover = CoefficientProver::new(\nL261:         &evaluator,\nL262:         tablewise,\nL263:         pairwise,\nL264:     );\nL265:     let proof = sumcheck(\nL266:         &mut prover,\nL267:         num_rounds,\nL268:         &mut transcript,\nL269:         noop_hook,\nL270:     );\nL271: ### Verification\nL272: One verifier for any degree $d$. Returns `SumcheckResult { challenges, final_claim }` — ⚠️ the caller is responsible for the oracle check (【97†Thaler Remark 4.2†people.cs.georgetown.edu】).\nL273: \nL274:     use effsc::{noop_hook_verify, verifier::sumcheck_verify};\nL275: \nL276:     let result = sumcheck_verify(\nL277:         claimed_sum,\nL278:         degree,\nL279:         num_rounds,\nL280:         &mut transcript,\nL281:         noop_hook_verify,\nL282:     )?;\nL283: \nL284:     // Standalone: compare against the prover's claimed final value.\nL285:     assert_eq!(result.final_claim, proof.final_value);\nL286: \nL287:     // Composed (WHIR, GKR): pass final_claim to the next layer.\nL288:     next_layer_claim = result.final_claim;\nL289: ## Variable Ordering\nL290: \nL291: Each prover comes in two variants:\nL292: \nL293:   * MSB (half-split) — optimal memory layout in most cases. Used by WHIR and WARP.\nL294:   * LSB (pair-split) — optimal for streaming applications where evaluations arrive sequentially.\nL295: MSB  | LSB\nL296: --- | ---\nL297: `MultilinearProver`  | `MultilinearProverLSB`\nL298: `InnerProductProver`  | `InnerProductProverLSB`\nL299: `CoefficientProver`  | `CoefficientProverLSB`\nL300: `EqFactoredProver`  | —\nL301: `GkrProver`  | —\nL302: \nL303: See 【98†`docs/design.md`】 for details.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nA Time-Space Tradeoff for the Sumcheck Prover (https://eprint.iacr.org/2024/524)\nciteturn1view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://eprint.iacr.org/2024/524\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 77\nL0: # What a lovely hat\nL1: #### Is it made out of 【0†tin foil†iacr.org】?\nL2: \nL3: 【1†Cryptology ePrint Archive】\nL4: \nL5:   * 【2†Papers】 Updates from the last:\nL6:     * 【3†7 days】\nL7:     * 【4†31 days】\nL8:     * 【5†6 months】\nL9:     * 【6†365 days】\nL10:     * * * *\nL11: \nL12:     * 【7†Listing by year】\nL13:     * 【8†All papers】\nL14:     * 【9†Compact view】\nL15:     * 【10†Subscribe†www.iacr.org】\nL16:     * * * *\nL17: \nL18:     * 【11†How to cite】\nL19:     * * * *\nL20:     * 【12†Harvesting metadata】\nL21:   * 【2†Submissions】\nL22:     * 【13†Submit a paper】\nL23:     * 【14†Revise or withdraw a paper】\nL24:     * 【15†Acceptance and publishing conditions】\nL25: \nL26: 【16†Image: Search Button】\nL27: \nL28: [Input] [Button: Search ]\nL29: #### Paper 2024/524\nL30: \nL31: ### 【17†A Time-Space Tradeoff for the Sumcheck Prover】\nL32: \nL33: Alessandro Chiesa, École Polytechnique Fédérale de Lausanne\nL34: \nL35: Elisabetta Fedele, ETH Zurich\nL36: \nL37: Giacomo Fenzi, École Polytechnique Fédérale de Lausanne\nL38: \nL39: Andrew Zitek-Estrada, École Polytechnique Fédérale de Lausanne\nL40: ##### Abstract\nL41: The sumcheck protocol is an interactive protocol for verifying the sum of a low-degree polynomial over a hypercube. This protocol is widely used in practice, where an efficient implementation of the (honest) prover algorithm is paramount. Prior work contributes highly-efficient prover algorithms for the notable special case of multilinear polynomials (and related settings).\nL42: [CTY11] presents two algorithms, the first of which uses logarithmic space but runs in superlinear time; the latter runs in linear time but uses linear space. In this short note, we present a family of prover algorithms for the multilinear sumcheck protocol that offer new time-space tradeoffs. In particular, we recover the aforementioned algorithms as special cases.\nL43: Moreover, we provide an efficient implementation of the new algorithms, and our experiments show that the asymptotics translate into new concrete efficiency tradeoffs\nL44: Note: Update acks and related works.\nL45: ##### Metadata\nL46: \nL47: Available format(s)\nL48:     【17†PDF】\nL49: Category\nL50:     【18†Cryptographic protocols】\nL51: Publication info\nL52:     Preprint.\nL53: Contact author(s)\nL54:     alessandro chiesa @ epfl ch\nL55: efedele @ ethz ch\nL56: giacomo fenzi @ epfl ch\nL57: andrew zitek @ epfl ch\nL58: History\nL59:     2024-09-11: last of 2 revisions\nL60:     2024-04-03: received\nL61:     【19†See all versions】\nL62: Short URL\nL63:     【20†https://ia.cr/2024/524†ia.cr】\nL64: License\nL65: \nL66: 【21†CC BY†creativecommons.org】\nL67: BibTeX 【22†Image】Copy to clipboard\nL68: \nL69:     @misc{cryptoeprint:2024/524,\nL70:           author = {Alessandro Chiesa and Elisabetta Fedele and Giacomo Fenzi and Andrew Zitek-Estrada},\nL71:           title = {A Time-Space Tradeoff for the Sumcheck Prover},\nL72:           howpublished = {Cryptology {ePrint} Archive, Paper 2024/524},\nL73:           year = {2024},\nL74:           url = {https://eprint.iacr.org/2024/524}\nL75:     }\nL76: Note: In order to protect the privacy of readers, eprint.iacr.org does not use cookies or embedded third party content.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nReleases · succinctlabs/sp1 · GitHub (https://github.com/succinctlabs/sp1/releases)\nciteturn1view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://github.com/succinctlabs/sp1/releases\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 851\nL0: 【0†Skip to content】\nL1: ## 